      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The item calls false the claim that the Borel-representative discipline is unnecessary because the continuous precompositions used in convolution preserve Lebesgue measurability. Its cited counterexample only gives a particular continuous map f=psi^{-1} for which a null set has a nonmeasurable preimage. It does not apply to convolution's maps. For T(x,y)=(x-y,y), T is an invertible linear map and T^{-1}(N) is null whenever N is Lebesgue null; likewise translations and coordinate projections preserve completed-Lebesgue measurability. Thus arbitrary Lebesgue measurable f,g still give measurable f(x-y)g(y), so the displayed claim is true for the convolution formula.",
